# TAS project handoff — September 21, 2026

## Latest implementation: assisted play in the Ravel browser

User requested switching from Evades to visible, assisted Ravel play. Implemented `npm run ravel:play` (separate `.ravel-browser-profile`) and `npm run ravel:play -- --attach` (existing Ravel tab only, preserving Evades tabs/session). Equivalent CLI flag: `--game ravel`. Current Ravel profile **`ravel-assisted-v1`**, revision **`4db5225f9add`**; shared CLI/control changes also change the Evades hash to `981e153bf18f`, without adding new Evades enemy mechanics.

Browser mode supports **Candy and Basic/Normal**, **FPS Limit 60**, **Input/Tick Delay 0**, normal game speed, mouse steering inactive, and default arrows/Shift/X/1. P toggles assistance; Escape/WASD pauses for manual play; native R/T/E/End/safepoint actions pause first and propagate to the game. R is not rescue. Death contact is captured before Easy respawn and assistance remains paused until P. Auto Candy and available speed upgrades remain; attaching does not reset areas, grant stats, or change settings. A second Ravel controller is rejected, reload starts paused, Ctrl+C releases keys and restores instrumentation. See `docs/ravel-assist.md` for setup and scope.

`src/ravel/observe.js` extracts the existing observation conversion from the VM bridge, shared by both environments. Offline spawn IDs/reset/replay behavior is preserved and source hashing includes the extracted module. `src/ravel/browser.js` observes current native state, wraps update/kill boundaries to count/timestamp ticks and retain a death snapshot, and calls original engine methods without advancing extra simulation steps. Observation failure cannot suppress native death. Browser key events dispatch at document level, matching Ravel's native listeners. The CLI uses the accepted policy settings and Ravel models/phase telemetry, longer giant/Slippery horizons and cadence alignment, with actual computation/input overhead and **zero simulated network delay**. The Ravel hero/config guards pause unsupported states. Evades remains the default for existing `npm run baseline` commands.

All **175 tests pass**, including real isolated Chrome tests for keys, Candy, P/Escape/WASD, R, Easy-death/retry, observation-error handling, configuration guards, duplicate attachment, reload and cleanup, plus model equivalence with the offline runner. A full CLI/CDP smoke test in isolated Chrome at **MM420, seed20001, Candy/max stats**, ran the real browser loop and reached **421 with zero deaths** and 660 speed. It stopped at that first transition; no full Ravel run or user browser interaction occurred. That smoke revision was `3726ec7e3d99`; the final revision adds observation-error protection and help/formatting corrections, with the complete suite rerun. Artifacts are in `artifacts/ravel-assist/`. Scripts checked from the public Ravel site matched the pinned main/listeners/game/entities/utils source.

## Previous investigation: late-evening game lag

User suspects the server is laggier than earlier in the day and asked about settings. Latest `death-1790058622764.json` is WW7/Wavy546, still revision `cead0cca1cc2`. Fresh-update gaps reach 124.3 ms in its final 3.92 seconds, while browser reads/input calls are roughly 4.16/0.82 ms median. The preceding two late-evening deaths have 432/550 ms gaps versus roughly 38–80 ms in earlier WW samples. Packet count catches up at about 60/s overall. Evidence supports update-delivery jitter but cannot isolate server from network/client buffering; ping/server processing telemetry is absent. Wavy prediction remains a separate gap.

At inspection two attached CLI processes existed: PID 41598 older `cead0cca1cc2`, PID 42156 updated `699761930e1e`. The new process stayed paused throughout the overlapping run; the older one produced the death. Do not describe it as a failed live test of the WW19 fix or proven fighting active controllers. No processes were stopped, browser attached, game settings changed, or runtime code edited in this investigation. `artifacts/lag-settings-review.json` and the newest `docs/recent-deaths.md` section contain metrics/settings/source evidence. Recommended manual checks: one updated controller only; enable in-game Display FPS/Ping and compare available servers; reduce visual effects only for local rendering cost; disable Chrome Energy Saver while playing. Client prediction does not fix missing authoritative packets. Runtime remains `699761930e1e`.

## Latest update: WW19 Zoning and duplicate-packet control

Latest user: **"i just died i think WW bballs are not good"**. Investigated `artifacts/death-1790057484504.json`: WW19, revision `cead0cca1cc2`, Zoning enemy 457/type249, final overlap 3.61 units. The enemy decelerated at 450 units/s², but was projected at constant velocity. Packet 5806 also repeated for roughly 245 ms; the CLI held up-right and missed its retained up turn. The first new packet was already too late to escape.

Current revision **`699761930e1e`**, profile still **`ravel-transfer-v1`**. Restart `npm run baseline -- --attach`, then P. `MotionTracker` fits Zoning deceleration only from two matching past intervals with position validation; `live-models.js` updates speed before movement and samples native ticks. New `PacketClock` preserves the first capture of a repeated packet. The CLI rechecks repeats after 25 ms using an 8 ms optional-search budget and includes inputs sent after that original capture; fresh packets retain 18 ms. Logs add `packetAgeMs` and `stateAt`. The hard 750 ms stale pause remains. No live browser was attached or controlled, and no Ravel run was started.

All **171 tests pass**. The independent 0.3-second recorded-position gap replay reproduces the old collision at +1 tick command arrival. Corrected control survives −1/0/+1 tick arrivals with full forward progress (207 units each), minimum clearance 25.03 units and two turns. An exhausted optional-search budget also survives. Ten repetitions per timing with an 8 ms budget give 30/30 survivals and identical full forward progress; median local search 6.35 ms, p95 7.41, maximum 8.06 (separate from fixed 13 ms simulated compute/delivery). Six OOH10/Cata35 windows match pre-fix route metrics exactly. Artifacts and source backup are in `artifacts/ww19-fix/`; fixture/test in `test/fixtures/ww19-zoning.json` and `test/ww19.test.js`. These are short replay checks, not a live whole-level result.

Remaining gaps: Zoning's stop/next perpendicular turn is not observable from current public telemetry; forecasts hold the stop position after confirmed deceleration. Wavy phase timing remains unresolved (older WW48 `death-1790056412813.json` is a separate Wavy failure). Do not revive the broad Wacky learner. Preserve older rollback/rescue/Ravel work and the user's preference to start future Ravel attempts near 420. See the newest `docs/recent-deaths.md` section for the complete evidence and replay scope.

## Previous update: Ravel improvements ported to live Evades

Previous user request: **port this over to regular Evades**. Implemented as the default live CLI profile `ravel-transfer-v1`, then runtime revision `cead0cca1cc2`. Restart with `npm run baseline -- --attach`, then P. No live browser was controlled and no further full Ravel run was started. Preserve the user's preference to start near 420 for future Ravel retries.

`src/live-models.js` adapts the transferable mechanics to production rules: Icicle countdown and both-axis wall reversal; native Turning; candidate-dependent Liquid; Slippery lock/steering/wall boost/Candy expiry; approximate impending Ice shots toward us and visible teammates; longer giant-Dasher/Slippery horizons; measured command cadence; backward-exit planning barriers. `observe.js` exports the corresponding telemetry and keeps freeze projectiles. The CLI prepares models before navigation and local planning, retains raw observations in traces, logs `planning` options/model counts, and hashes the new module. P/Escape/R, Candy, upgrades, and latency controls remain. Ordinary live Dashers retain their established behavior: a broader coarse-sampling change hurt progress in a delayed Cata replay and was restricted to giant bodies. The planner's wall test now rejects disjoint bounds early; 100,000 generated comparisons match the old checker.

All 165 tests pass, including independent public-client prediction functions in `test/fixtures/live-client-models.json`. The inspected production bundle SHA-256 is `cb71f0d3aca1be25961c7e9bc3f789d9863e545753da65d996422fe3577c57b2`, identical to the existing catalog. Both profiles survive six OOH 10 / Cata 35 recorded windows with equal progress, clearance, turns and Shift fractions. Validation artifacts and the pre-port snapshot are under `artifacts/live-port/`; they are ignored by Git. Interleaved total-planning medians were 11.96→15.71 ms on OOH and 18.08→18.17 ms on Cata. This port adds prediction coverage, not a proven overall latency reduction. Local search still uses an 18 ms optional-work budget; navigation and GC can exceed it. Latency calibration pauses near Slippery fields so locked steering cannot corrupt the delay fit. Recorded-position replay explicitly rejects new player-dependent/firing/Slippery encounters it cannot simulate independently.

Important gaps: no live full-course clear, Wavy phase telemetry unavailable, existing bounded Spiral fit retained, Ice firing/target selection remains approximate, other players are projected from current positions, and unknown phases/Sizing/other future projectiles remain unsupported. Do not import Ravel's Dasher order or hidden phase clocks into production. See `docs/live-port.md` for source, limitations and commands. `scripts/replay-encounter.js --live` and `scripts/profile-planner.js --live` now include live preparation for diagnostics. No commits were made; preserve earlier rollback/rescue/death changes already in this dirty working tree.

## Earlier update: offline Ravel training

Latest steering: **do not start another full run; start around area 420**. The prior objective was completing 480. Fresh attempt `mm-full-09/` cleared 1–449 and died in 450 after an Ice Sniper freeze. The following full attempt `mm-full-10/` was interrupted at the user's request; its report explicitly records that reason. Completed direct run: `artifacts/ravel/mm-420-480-01/`, continuous 420–480, seed 20001, Candy speed 17, 125 ms input + 50 ms fixed compute. Reached 481 with zero deaths in 405.9167 simulated seconds. `verification.json` confirms 62 consecutive visits and matching physical/full-observation replay. Revision `a6841643e4d2`; planning p95 52.80 ms, 514 measured overruns not inserted into simulation. Do not restart from area 1 without a new user request.

Native Dasher phase prediction and 1.5-second look-ahead for giant Dashers resolved the earlier area-360 traps. The new Ice Sniper model predicts candidate-dependent future firing from current clocks/range, with an eight-unit radius allowance for the two native first-update schedules. Forecasts whose earliest shot lies beyond the horizon are filtered out; ordinary shooter bodies remain lethal. It cleared the original 450 failure and continued to 481 in `ice450-prefix-01/`, and 14/14 targeted fresh cases passed in `ice-sniper-holdout-01/`. Native timing tests cover new/existing projectile groups and a shooter bouncing before firing. No other shooter families have the future-firing model yet.

The offline runner executes pinned Ravel physics in an isolated Node VM. It does not attach to Chrome or change the live policy. `npm run ravel:setup` installs/verifies the MIT source at commit `563f5434f68d07b2d70ede3f307f857157d42107`; `data/ravel-source.json` pins hashes of the five engine files. Seeded trials support continuous state, actual Candy casting/collection, delayed ordered inputs, jitter, first-death stopping and deterministic action replay. Default full-course evidence uses Candy, speed 17, maximum starting energy/regen/abilities, 125 ms input delay and 50 ms fixed compute allowance. Actual planning overruns are measured but not inserted into simulated delay. Do not claim real-time or live certification.

Added during full-course debugging: native Icicle pause/restart forecasts; Slippery steering lock, aura and wall recovery with two-second look-ahead; candidate-dependent Liquid acceleration; Wavy/Turning/Spiral phase curves; Dasher movement-before-phase-update and clamped wall contacts; three-tick action-segment alignment; native speed upgrades using available points while waiting in shelter. Ravel models use only current observation plus geometry, never future engine/RNG state. Optional planner callbacks leave ordinary live observations on existing paths. `test/fixtures/ravel-slip84.json` catches a previously accepted follow-up turn that could not arrive on the decision cadence.

All 153 tests pass with the pinned engine installed (`artifacts/ravel/tests-current.txt`). Targeted clears: Icicle 20/20; Slippery 20/20; Liquid 24/24; Wavy 10/10. Native trajectory agreement covers Wavy, Turning, Spiral and Dasher phase/collision behavior. Independent final-block testing cleared all 120 areas 361–480; area 465 needed 32.45 seconds after its initial 20-second timeout. A separate speed-13 fresh-start Slippery suite remains 9/10: area 324 seed 33001 dies after an unforecast first Candy pickup accelerates it into an aura. A prior suffix debug (`final-continuous-prefix-01/`) died in 455 when a new Ice Sniper projectile appeared 100 ms before freezing the player, inside the 175 ms response window; see `ice455-review.json`. Future firing by other projectile families, changing Sizing radii and several enemy phase transitions also remain model gaps. Do not claim perfect generalization.

`data/ravel-policy.json` retains numeric defaults and enables a planning barrier at backward portals. Original parameter training improved 22/24 to 24/24 training clears and 23/24 to 24/24 held-out clears, with no regressions. Later mechanic changes are source-derived model/planner improvements, not neural weight training. See `docs/ravel-training.md` for commands, assumptions and evidence.

Replay verification is complete; the command is `npm run ravel:verify -- --trace <trace.json> --output <verification.json>`. The verifier checks every consecutive visit in the requested range, zero deaths, physical replay equality and absence of a recorded prefix. `requestedRangeVerified` is separate from `fullCourseVerified` (all 480). Prefix debugging (`--prefix <trace> --resume-area N`) replays prior inputs through the native engine before resuming current planning; provenance is explicit. The checkpoint and training docs record the successful requested range. The subsequent live port is complete as described above. Artifacts are ignored by Git; preserve them separately. Preserve all live rollback, rescue and recorded-death work below.

